WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.034 --> 00:00:03.469
WIL WILLIS: Bladesmiths,
congratulations.

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:05.138
You've made it into the third
round of this competition,

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:07.339
and now it's time to send
you back to your home forges

00:00:07.439 --> 00:00:10.809
to recreate this iconic
weapon from history.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:11.544
That weapon is--

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:15.782
Charlemagne's Joyeuse.

00:00:18.985 --> 00:00:19.919
Good luck.

00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:20.553
We'll see you in four days.

00:00:26.291 --> 00:00:27.527
MITCH: All right, it's day 1.

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:30.797
Today the plan is to get
the blade forged out.

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:32.498
This is definitely
out-of-the-box for me,

00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:35.668
so this will be the first sword
I ever completely assemble.

00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:39.505
We're going to start out with a
bar of 1075 high-carbon steel.

00:00:39.605 --> 00:00:41.274
We're going to do a lot
of the quick forging

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.375
to shape with a power hammer.

00:00:43.442 --> 00:00:45.845
Whenever you try to move a
big piece of metal like this,

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:49.448
it's quite a challenge
to keep it straight.

00:00:49.548 --> 00:00:50.650
Whoa.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:55.655
It's getting long and
heavy and hard to hold.

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:57.823
So end of day 1, good day.

00:00:57.923 --> 00:00:59.158
We've got the blade forged out.

00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:01.694
It's cooling down
between a couple of 2x6s,

00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:04.063
so that way it doesn't
warp as it cools down.

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:06.733
Tomorrow we'll get it
ready for heat treating.

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:11.537
PEYTON: I'm back here
in Valentine, Nebraska,

00:01:11.637 --> 00:01:14.073
and I'm here to make
Charlemagne's Joyeuse.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.142
Currently I've never
made a sword before.

00:01:16.208 --> 00:01:18.578
This is going to be an
interesting experiment for me.

00:01:18.678 --> 00:01:22.048
So I'm going to use a big piece
of 5160 bar stock that can take

00:01:22.114 --> 00:01:24.350
a lot of damage and flex well.

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:26.819
I want to make sure I got
the length of the blade done,

00:01:26.919 --> 00:01:28.821
down to a reasonable size.

00:01:28.921 --> 00:01:31.924
So this steel is definitely the
most awkward thing that I've

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:33.359
ever worked with in the shop.

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:35.528
It's like a big, wet noodle.

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:38.731
End of day 1, I've
got my blade profiled.

00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:41.467
Tomorrow the plan is I'm
going to start heat treating

00:01:41.568 --> 00:01:45.972 line:0%
and get this Damascus
ready for the cross-guard.

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:47.373 line:0%
MITCH: It's the
morning of day 2.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.076 line:0%
Now we're going to get
ready for heat treating.

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:52.645
The main thing is I'm
definitely paying really close

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:54.714
attention to the parameters.

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:56.583
Getting pretty close to
coming up in temperature,

00:01:56.649 --> 00:02:01.721
so we're about five minutes
away and we'll be quenching her.

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:02.722
All right, this is it.

00:02:05.891 --> 00:02:06.593
Eureka.

00:02:09.962 --> 00:02:13.432
All right.

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:14.700
Well.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.668
I'm worried that hitting the
bottom of the quench tank

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:18.704
did some damage.

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:20.039
Come on, man.

00:02:20.105 --> 00:02:22.742
I tried to fish it out,
but unfortunately I

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:24.277
didn't catch anything.

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:26.579
Ended up pouring it out in a
5-gallon bucket in my yard.

00:02:26.645 --> 00:02:29.749
I got the blade out,
so it's all good now.

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:33.185
I did pick up a slight
warp close to the tip.

00:02:33.286 --> 00:02:34.353
I should be able
to straighten that

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.055
out during the temper cycle.

00:02:36.121 --> 00:02:39.592 line:7%
So time will tell.

00:02:39.692 --> 00:02:41.460 line:0%
PEYTON: The morning
of day 3, plan today

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:44.897 line:0%
is to grind in my fullers
and get all my fittings

00:02:44.997 --> 00:02:46.565
ground down and shaped.

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:49.068
So my biggest fear of
putting in this fuller

00:02:49.135 --> 00:02:51.037
is just grinding right
through my blade.

00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:53.238
I can kind of tell
how close it is just

00:02:53.305 --> 00:02:55.108
by feeling it with my hand.

00:02:55.208 --> 00:02:57.009
After about an hour,
the fuller's done

00:02:57.075 --> 00:02:59.378
and everything is looking good.

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:00.780
Coming out pretty well.

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:03.248
Now I can work on profiling
the pommel and guard

00:03:03.348 --> 00:03:07.520
and getting them exactly
to where I want it.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.989 line:0%
For my handle I
choose diamond wood

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:12.058 line:0%
because it can
take a lot of abuse

00:03:12.158 --> 00:03:12.959 line:7%
and it's not going to break.

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:17.663
I'm in the homestretch now.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:20.333
Tomorrow I'm just going to be
focusing on getting everything

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:21.534
fitted and glued together.

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:25.404 line:0%
It's a good-looking
piece so far.

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:28.541 line:0%
MITCH: Morning of day 4, I
got a lot of work ahead of me.

00:03:28.607 --> 00:03:31.444
My biggest focus is going to
be getting the blade straight,

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:34.613
and so I want to get that out of
the way and then go from there.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.051
My solution is to extend
the width of the jaws

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:40.453
with some angle iron,
heat it up, and use

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.921
some wooden dowels to
put the proper pressure

00:03:42.988 --> 00:03:45.258
where it needs to be.

00:03:45.325 --> 00:03:46.459
She's straight.

00:03:46.525 --> 00:03:48.127
Oh, that's so freaking awesome.

00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:49.929
Now I need to make the handle.

00:03:49.996 --> 00:03:52.999
Time's running out, so
it's a race to the finish.

00:03:57.937 --> 00:03:58.938
She's getting there.

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:00.305
End of day 4.

00:04:00.372 --> 00:04:02.742
Man, it's been quite a trip.

00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:04.043
Everything turned out great.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.145
A lot of hard
work, but I believe

00:04:06.211 --> 00:04:08.147
it really paid off in the end.

00:04:08.247 --> 00:04:09.649
That's a sword in four days.

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:13.119
DOUG MARCAIDA: All
right, bladesmiths,

00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:16.322
welcome to the kill test.

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:18.191
To find out how lethal
your weapons are according

00:04:18.291 --> 00:04:19.892
to its historic design,
I will take your weapon,

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:23.830
deliver some killing slashes and
thrusts on this poor carcass.

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:25.564
Mitch, you're up first.
You ready for this?

00:04:25.631 --> 00:04:26.266
 Yes, sir.

00:04:30.003 --> 00:04:34.407
[music playing]

00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:48.787
 All right, Mitch, let's
talk about your sword here.

00:04:48.887 --> 00:04:52.325
Your edge coupled with the
weight that you have here

00:04:52.391 --> 00:04:55.828
allows for very deep
cuts with every strike.

00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:58.931
Overall, sir, it will kill.

00:04:59.031 --> 00:05:00.132
 Thank you.

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:01.267
 All right, Peyton,
it's your turn.

00:05:01.333 --> 00:05:02.435
You ready?
- Oh yeah.

00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:09.842
[music playing]

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:31.197
 All right, Peyton,
that was "joyeuse."

00:05:31.263 --> 00:05:32.631
What I like about
this sword is not

00:05:32.732 --> 00:05:34.801
just about the
edge or the point,

00:05:34.901 --> 00:05:35.968
it's the balance of this.

00:05:36.069 --> 00:05:41.073
I can actually use some
velocity and it cuts deep.

00:05:41.173 --> 00:05:43.709
Overall, sir, it will kill.

00:05:43.776 --> 00:05:44.410
 Thank you.

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:50.783
DAVE BAKER: All right,
gentlemen, it's time

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:51.917
for the strength test.

00:05:52.018 --> 00:05:54.087
Now, to test the strength
and overall construction

00:05:54.153 --> 00:05:56.055
of your blades, I
will be attacking

00:05:56.122 --> 00:05:58.557
our armored knights here.

00:05:58.657 --> 00:06:00.459
Now, this is not
about what your blades

00:06:00.525 --> 00:06:01.961
are going to do to that target.

00:06:02.027 --> 00:06:04.096
It's about what that target's
going to do your blades.

00:06:04.196 --> 00:06:04.964
Mitch, you're up first.

00:06:05.064 --> 00:06:05.665
You ready for this?

00:06:05.765 --> 00:06:06.999
 Yes, sir.

00:06:07.066 --> 00:06:11.871
[music playing]

00:06:20.612 --> 00:06:23.649
 I think I just peed a little.

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:26.552
 All right, Mitch, your edge
did take some damage here.

00:06:26.619 --> 00:06:30.322
You can see the chip right
there and some rolls.

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:32.458
And your sword is heavy.

00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:35.928
You know, this is a lot of
weight for a single-hand sword.

00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:37.396
More in the range of
a two-handed sword

00:06:37.496 --> 00:06:38.597
as far as the weight goes.

00:06:38.697 --> 00:06:42.468
But she held up,
she's in one piece.

00:06:42.535 --> 00:06:43.369
Good job, Mitch.

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:44.569
 Thank you.

00:06:44.669 --> 00:06:46.038
 All right, Peyton, you're up.

00:06:46.105 --> 00:06:47.239
You ready?

00:06:47.306 --> 00:06:47.940
 Yeah.

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:55.815
[music playing]

00:07:05.091 --> 00:07:06.458
All right.

00:07:06.525 --> 00:07:08.894
It's weighty, but the weight
is all back in my hands

00:07:08.994 --> 00:07:12.531
so it doesn't feel as
heavy in the blade.

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:14.533
This sword has a much
better point of balance

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:15.934
than the other sword.

00:07:16.001 --> 00:07:18.304
Your blade did take some
chips and some edge rolling,

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:20.873
but I think your
grinds are beautiful,

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.309
the sword is all solid.

00:07:23.376 --> 00:07:24.143
You did a good job.

00:07:24.243 --> 00:07:24.911
 Thank you.

00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:31.517
DOUG MARCAIDA: All
right, bladesmiths,

00:07:31.617 --> 00:07:36.255
welcome to the sharpness
test, the sandbag gauntlet.

00:07:36.355 --> 00:07:37.690
To test the sharpness
of your weapon,

00:07:37.756 --> 00:07:39.592
I will slash across these bags.

00:07:39.692 --> 00:07:41.494
Now, unlike the strength,
this is all about how sharp

00:07:41.561 --> 00:07:43.863
your blades are and how
well they cut these bags.

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:45.230
Mitch, you're up first.
You ready?

00:07:45.297 --> 00:07:45.998
 Yes, sir.

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:54.006
[music playing]

00:08:03.216 --> 00:08:04.450
 All right, Mitch,
so despite some

00:08:04.517 --> 00:08:05.917
of the damage of
rolls and chips,

00:08:06.018 --> 00:08:08.854
it wasn't an issue at all
in cutting these bags.

00:08:08.954 --> 00:08:09.722
It will cut.

00:08:09.822 --> 00:08:10.722
 Thank you.

00:08:10.789 --> 00:08:11.991
 All right, Peyton,
your turn, sir.

00:08:12.058 --> 00:08:12.725
Are you ready?

00:08:12.792 --> 00:08:13.425
 Yes, sir.

00:08:13.526 --> 00:08:17.563
[music playing]

00:08:31.743 --> 00:08:33.312
All right, Peyton, the
handle construction

00:08:33.379 --> 00:08:36.682
is smooth but ovoid enough to
where I wrap my hand around it

00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:38.416
and I get a very good grip.

00:08:38.483 --> 00:08:39.184
It will cut.

00:08:39.251 --> 00:08:41.554
 Thank you.

00:08:41.621 --> 00:08:43.789
WIL WILLIS: Mitch, Peyton,
the judges' deliberation

00:08:43.856 --> 00:08:45.157
is complete.

00:08:45.223 --> 00:08:47.025
You guys have done fantastic
work on these finale weapons,

00:08:47.125 --> 00:08:49.929
but there can only be one
"Forged in Fire" champion.

00:08:50.029 --> 00:08:52.064
Our new "Forged in
Fire" champion is--

00:08:55.534 --> 00:08:56.669
Peyton, congratulations.

00:08:56.735 --> 00:08:58.170
You're the "Forged
in Fire" champion.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:01.140
Mitch, unfortunately your
blade didn't make the cut.

00:09:01.207 --> 00:09:03.342
 Mitch, you brought us a
sword that performed well

00:09:03.442 --> 00:09:06.245
in all three of our tests, so
this came down to the finer

00:09:06.312 --> 00:09:08.413
points of craftsmanship
as in where

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:10.349
to place the balance
point on that blade

00:09:10.449 --> 00:09:12.017
and the general weight.

00:09:12.084 --> 00:09:14.353
That balance point being so far
forward made it harder to wield

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:15.554
and harder to
control, and that's

00:09:15.654 --> 00:09:16.856
why we're letting you go.

00:09:16.923 --> 00:09:17.890
 I understand.

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:19.424
 Mitch, you've been
a great competitor

00:09:19.491 --> 00:09:21.159
this entire competition,
but at this time,

00:09:21.226 --> 00:09:24.730
I have to ask you to please
surrender your blade.

00:09:24.796 --> 00:09:26.131
MITCH: Obviously was
a little disappointed.

00:09:26.232 --> 00:09:28.867
Being my first sword,
nothing to compare it to.

00:09:28.967 --> 00:09:30.603
I thought the weight felt good.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:32.772
I tried to keep the 6-
to 8-inches balance point

00:09:32.838 --> 00:09:34.840
that I was told
normally swords carry.

00:09:34.907 --> 00:09:37.543
So overall, happy
with what I turned in

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:39.044
and I'm happy for Peyton.

00:09:39.144 --> 00:09:41.347
If I had to lose to anyone,
I'd just as soon lose to him.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:45.784
 Peyton, congratulations.

00:09:45.851 --> 00:09:47.986
You are the new "Forged
in Fire" champion,

00:09:48.053 --> 00:09:52.157
and that is a title that comes
with a check for $10,000.

00:09:52.224 --> 00:09:53.458
Congratulations.

00:09:53.525 --> 00:09:56.561
Please present your
sword to the judges.

00:09:56.628 --> 00:09:58.464
PEYTON: I'm the next
"Forged in Fire" champion.

00:09:58.564 --> 00:10:00.099
Aw, it's just crazy.

00:10:00.166 --> 00:10:02.267
As soon as I heard my name, it
was just like a weight just got

00:10:02.334 --> 00:10:03.302
pulled off my chest.

00:10:03.368 --> 00:10:07.473
Just like-- [sigh] it's over.

00:10:07.573 --> 00:10:09.342
Finally can breathe.

